From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 13:15:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 24/32] msgs: store long message text in full, don't truncate
 on write
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Core truncated message text to 38 lines x 100 chars when writing it to
`msgs.txt` and stashed the full text in `mime_headers` behind a fake
"Content-Type: text/plain" part, so a UI could offer it as an HTML
message. That made long messages uneditable ("Cannot edit HTML
messages"), stored every long body twice, and conflated "long text" with
"this mail had HTML".

Drop the truncation from both write paths — `MimeMessage` (incoming) and
`Chat::prepare_msg_raw` (outgoing) — together with the `is_mime_modified`
flag it set and the fabricated mime_headers part. `truncate_msg_text` and
`truncate_by_lines` have no callers left and are deleted; `truncate`
stays, it is what summaries use. Clamping long text for display is now
the UI's job (CSS), which is where it belongs.

Backup compatibility: the schema is unchanged, only which value lands in
the existing `msgs.txt` column. The resulting row shape — full text in
txt, mime_modified=0, empty mime_headers — is exactly what official core
already writes for bot accounts and for edited messages on the sender's
device, so old and new rows are the same shape. Rows written by official
core are never rewritten: `MsgId::get_html()` and src/html.rs are
untouched, so messages already in a database keep their truncated text
plus stashed MIME and still open in the HTML viewer.

Removing the write-time truncation also removes the implicit bound every
string derived from `msg.text` relied on, so the derivations that are
previews are now bounded explicitly:

- `Message::get_summary_text_without_prefix()` truncates to
  DC_DESIRED_TEXT_LEN before concatenating and collapsing whitespace.
  This one line restores the exact ceiling `Summary::text` had before for
  every consumer (chatlist, notifications, reactions, FFI) and keeps the
  whitespace pass from allocating two copies of a megabyte body.
- `Message::set_quote()` bounds the quoted text the same way. It is the
  single funnel all quote producers pass through, and the only place
  where an unbounded value would go out on the wire.
- jsonrpc chatlist items, notification bodies and search results clamp to
  500 chars: 3800 per list row is still the wrong payload for a preview.
  `tools::truncate` becomes `pub` so jsonrpc can use it and search hits
  stay verbatim text rather than becoming summaries.
- The maps webxdc POI label clamps to one line.

Deliberately not copied from upstream's draft: it truncates in
`Message::load_from_db` behind a context flag. That silently shortens
drafts, saved messages and resends — the text the user is still editing
is not a preview — so the bound belongs at each preview site instead.
